::error file=fixtures/vulnerable/WT001_missing_signer/vault.rs,line=37,col=9,title=WT001 critical::`Withdraw.authority` is verified by a `has_one` constraint but is never required to sign, and no account in `Withdraw` signs at all. The constraint proves which account this is; it does not prove the holder authorised anything. Public keys are public, so any caller can pass this one.%0A%0AFix: Type the account as `Signer<'info>`. If it must stay an `AccountInfo`, require the signature explicitly with `#[account(signer)]` or `constraint = authority.is_signer`.%0A%0Aconfidence high · id 2d70e5e62f325c65
::error file=fixtures/vulnerable/WT002_missing_owner/oracle.rs,line=44,col=9,title=WT002 critical::`Borrow.oracle` is an unvalidated account whose data is deserialised in `borrow` without establishing the owning program. An attacker can pass an account owned by their own program with any contents they like.%0A%0AFix: Use `Account<'info, T>`, which verifies the owner and discriminator before the handler runs. If the account must stay an `AccountInfo`, pin it with `#[account(owner = expected::ID)]` or assert `*account.owner` before reading its data.%0A%0Aconfidence medium · id ffe1e9d53c4e3354
::error file=fixtures/vulnerable/WT007_arbitrary_cpi/router.rs,line=55,col=9,title=WT007 critical::`Forward.target_program` is invoked as a program by `forward`, but its address is never established. The caller chooses which program receives the invocation, and any signing seeds this program supplies go with it.%0A%0AFix: Declare the callee as `Program<'info, T>`, or `Interface<'info, T>` where several implementations are accepted. Where no Anchor type exists, pin the address with `#[account(address = expected::ID)]`.%0A%0Aconfidence high · id 2f064f6836edaf41
::error file=fixtures/vulnerable/WT003_unchecked_arithmetic/staking.rs,line=23,col=24,title=WT003 high::`stake.amount + amount` in `deposit` uses `+` on a value that holds funds. In a release build this wraps silently rather than failing.%0A%0AFix: Use `checked_add`/`checked_sub`/`checked_mul` and return an error on overflow, or `saturating_*` where clamping is the intended behaviour. Alternatively set `overflow-checks = true` under `[profile.release]`, which turns wrapping into a panic and silences this rule.%0A%0Aconfidence medium · id d2c5e1a8cb484e2e
::error file=fixtures/vulnerable/WT003_unchecked_arithmetic/staking.rs,line=24,col=9,title=WT003 high::`stake.total_deposited += amount` in `deposit` uses `+=` on a value that holds funds. In a release build this wraps silently rather than failing.%0A%0AFix: Use `checked_add`/`checked_sub`/`checked_mul` and return an error on overflow, or `saturating_*` where clamping is the intended behaviour. Alternatively set `overflow-checks = true` under `[profile.release]`, which turns wrapping into a panic and silences this rule.%0A%0Aconfidence medium · id edb68547df2c0203
::error file=fixtures/vulnerable/WT003_unchecked_arithmetic/staking.rs,line=27,col=29,title=WT003 high::`pool.total_staked + amount` in `deposit` uses `+` on a value that holds funds. In a release build this wraps silently rather than failing.%0A%0AFix: Use `checked_add`/`checked_sub`/`checked_mul` and return an error on overflow, or `saturating_*` where clamping is the intended behaviour. Alternatively set `overflow-checks = true` under `[profile.release]`, which turns wrapping into a panic and silences this rule.%0A%0Aconfidence medium · id 8855ca683806d7ee
::error file=fixtures/vulnerable/WT003_unchecked_arithmetic/staking.rs,line=36,col=23,title=WT003 high::`stake.amount * ctx.accounts.pool.reward_rate * elapsed as...` in `claim` uses `*` on a value that holds funds. In a release build this wraps silently rather than failing.%0A%0AFix: Use `checked_add`/`checked_sub`/`checked_mul` and return an error on overflow, or `saturating_*` where clamping is the intended behaviour. Alternatively set `overflow-checks = true` under `[profile.release]`, which turns wrapping into a panic and silences this rule.%0A%0Aconfidence medium · id 4d4451207675e9ee
::error file=fixtures/vulnerable/WT003_unchecked_arithmetic/staking.rs,line=38,col=9,title=WT003 high::`stake.pending_rewards += rewards` in `claim` uses `+=` on a value that holds funds. In a release build this wraps silently rather than failing.%0A%0AFix: Use `checked_add`/`checked_sub`/`checked_mul` and return an error on overflow, or `saturating_*` where clamping is the intended behaviour. Alternatively set `overflow-checks = true` under `[profile.release]`, which turns wrapping into a panic and silences this rule.%0A%0Aconfidence medium · id a7d974c1d5c33e83
::error file=fixtures/vulnerable/WT003_unchecked_arithmetic/staking.rs,line=41,col=9,title=WT003 high::`ctx.accounts.pool.remaining_rewards -= rewards` in `claim` uses `-=` on a value that holds funds. In a release build this wraps silently rather than failing.%0A%0AFix: Use `checked_add`/`checked_sub`/`checked_mul` and return an error on overflow, or `saturating_*` where clamping is the intended behaviour. Alternatively set `overflow-checks = true` under `[profile.release]`, which turns wrapping into a panic and silences this rule.%0A%0Aconfidence medium · id 22ba41310ec76064
::error file=fixtures/vulnerable/WT004_reinitialisation/registry.rs,line=37,col=9,title=WT004 high::`CreateProfile.profile` uses `init_if_needed` on program state, and no handler checks whether the account already holds data. A caller can pass an existing account and have the handler overwrite state that belongs to someone else.%0A%0AFix: Ask whether the account is already initialised before writing the fields that establish ownership — compare the stored authority against `Pubkey::default()`, or require it to match the caller. Where creation and update are genuinely different operations, give them separate instructions and use plain `init`.%0A%0Aconfidence medium · id 410ab9cbe9e9fbf2
::error file=fixtures/vulnerable/WT005_missing_has_one/treasury.rs,line=33,col=9,title=WT005 high::`Treasury` stores `authority: Pubkey`, and `Withdraw` takes both `treasury` and `authority`, but never checks that they match. The stored key records a relationship the instruction does not enforce, so any `treasury` can be used with any `authority`.%0A%0AFix: Add `has_one = <field>` to the account that stores the key, or assert it explicitly with `constraint = account.field == other.key()`. Deriving the account's address from the key with `seeds` enforces the same relationship and is stronger still.%0A%0Aconfidence medium · id 0dc13ff4575e2169
::error file=fixtures/vulnerable/WT006_non_canonical_bump/market.rs,line=38,col=9,title=WT006 high::`OpenMarket.market` derives its address with `bump = market_bump`, which comes from the instruction argument `market_bump`. The caller chooses the bump, so they can derive several valid addresses for the same seeds. Use a bare `bump` and let Anchor supply the canonical one.%0A%0AFix: Write `bump` on its own and let Anchor derive the canonical bump, storing `ctx.bumps.<account>` if later instructions need it. Where a bump is re-derived, read it from the account (`bump = account.bump`) rather than from the caller.%0A%0Aconfidence high · id f8c3eb4d2afe9cab
::error file=fixtures/vulnerable/WT009_sysvar_spoofing/clocked.rs,line=43,col=9,title=WT009 high::`Claim.clock` is named for a sysvar but is an unchecked account with no address constraint. Sysvars live at fixed addresses and are otherwise ordinary accounts, so a caller can substitute one with contents of their choosing.%0A%0AFix: Use `Sysvar<'info, Clock>` and its siblings, which assert the address. For sysvars Anchor has no type for, pin it with `#[account(address = sysvar::instructions::ID)]`.%0A%0Aconfidence high · id 54d2cd9bdf500a57
::error file=fixtures/vulnerable/WT010_unchecked_deserialisation/config_store.rs,line=21,col=12,title=WT010 high::`read_config` calls `try_deserialize_unchecked`, which does not verify the account discriminator. Any account owned by this program can then be read as this type, regardless of what it actually is.%0A%0AFix: Use `try_deserialize`, which verifies the discriminator, or the typed `Account<'info, T>` which does it before the handler runs. The `_unchecked` variants are for accounts you have just created and know the layout of.%0A%0Aconfidence high · id 1e42fc9635a63add
::warning file=fixtures/vulnerable/WT008_unsafe_close/closer.rs,line=23,col=12,title=WT008 medium::`close_position` sets an account's lamports to zero without clearing its data. The runtime only reclaims the account at the end of the transaction, so a caller can send lamports back within the same transaction and keep the account, its data intact.%0A%0AFix: Prefer Anchor's `close = destination` constraint, which drains the lamports, zeroes the data, and assigns the account to the system program. Closing by hand requires all three — zeroing the balance alone leaves the account revivable within the same transaction.%0A%0Aconfidence medium · id 8af310d24a646694
::warning file=fixtures/vulnerable/WT011_duplicate_mutable/transfer.rs,line=34,col=9,title=WT011 medium::`Transfer` takes 2 mutable `Balance` accounts (from, to) with nothing asserting they differ. A caller can pass the same address for all of them; Anchor deserialises it once per field, and only the last write survives.%0A%0AFix: Assert that the accounts differ: `constraint = first.key() != second.key()`. Anchor cannot infer it, because passing the same account twice is legitimate in other instructions.%0A%0Aconfidence medium · id ed7f4c41ecffac78
::warning file=fixtures/vulnerable/WT012_alloc_in_loop/rebalance.rs,line=23,col=28,title=WT012 low::`pool.weights.clone()` in `rebalance` allocates on every iteration. Hoist it out of the loop, or borrow instead — compute units are a hard per-transaction budget.%0A%0AFix: Hoist the allocation out of the loop, or borrow instead of cloning. Where the collection is only read, iterate over a reference.%0A%0Aconfidence medium · id 61d128c53432a529
